<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="stearate biosynthesis II (bacteria and plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5989" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: STEARIC_ACID Stearate| is a saturated fatty acid that occurs in many animal and vegetable fats and oils. It is an important constituent of milk fats, lard, and cocoa and shea butters. Stearate was first described by Michel Eugene Chevreul in 1823, and its name comes from Greek for &quot;hard fat&quot;, reflecting the fact that stearate forms a waxy solid |CITS: [16477801]|. &lt;i&gt;De novo&lt;/i&gt; plant fatty acid synthesis occurs primarily in plastids and chloroplasts (it was shown that at least in Arabidopsis fatty acid synthesis also occurs in the mitochondria). The process involves a series of reactions catalyzed by fatty acid synthase (FAS) complexes that provoke the condensation of |FRAME: MALONYL-ACP| and |FRAME: ACYL-ACP| (see |FRAME: FASYN-ELONG-PWY|). In animals the last [acp]-bound intermediate appears to be |FRAME: Palmitoyl-ACPs &quot;palmitoyl-[acp]&quot;|, and elongation to |FRAME: STEARIC_ACID| proceeds via CoA-bound intermediates in the ER (see |FRAME: PWY-5972|). In bacteria and plants elongation to stearate appears to continue via [acp]-bound intermediates, and |FRAME: Stearoyl-ACPs &quot;stearoyl-[acp]&quot;| has been detected in many species |CITS: [4831331]|. At least in plants, the elongation from |FRAME: Palmitoyl-ACPs &quot;palmitoyl-[acp]&quot;| to |FRAME: Stearoyl-ACPs &quot;stearoyl-[acp]&quot;| is catalyzed by a dedicated &amp;beta;-ketoacyl-[acp] synthase, encoded by |FRAME: G-10790| (The enzyme involved in &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; fatty acid synthesis system up to |FRAME: Palmitoyl-ACPs &quot;palmitoyl-[acp]&quot;| is encoded by |FRAME: AT5G46290|) |CITS: [4153356][11337402]|. |FRAME: Stearoyl-ACPs &quot;Strearoyl-[acp]&quot;| can be hydrolyzed by acyl-ACP thioesterase enzymes |CITS: [7734968]|. However, before the fatty acids can be exported out of the chloroplast they must be activated to fatty acyl-CoAs by an outer envelope |FRAME: AT1G77590| activity. Once activated, they are exported to the endoplasmic reticulum |CITS: [14973169]|. A more common route involves desaturation of |FRAME: STEARIC_ACID| to |FRAME: OLEATE-CPD|. Plants contain acyl-ACP desaturase enzymes, which introduce a double bond in the 9 position of |FRAME: Stearoyl-ACPs &quot;stearoyl-[acp]&quot;| to generate |FRAME: Oleoyl-ACPs &quot;oleoyl-[acp]&quot;| (see |FRAME: PWY-5147|). Stearate is the only fatty acid that is desaturated in its ACP-bound form. All other desaturation reactions occur only after the fatty acid has been attached to a glycerolipid backbone. In cyanobacteria stearate is also often desaturated to oleate. However, unlike plants, in cyanobacteria this desaturation reaction occurs only after the incorporation of stearate into a lipid. Prior to incorporation into a lipid, the fatty acid is transferred to a coenzyme A molecule, resulting in formation of |FRAME: STEAROYL-COA|. Since the activity of the desaturase enzymes is often very high, |FRAME: OLEATE-CPD| is often the main product of intraplastidial fatty acid synthesis |CITS: [8652653]|.</Description>
