      <Comment>Stearate is a saturated fatty acid that occurs in many animal and vegetable fats and oils. It is a large constituent in milk fats, lard, and cocoa and shea butters. The name comes from Greek for &quot;hard fat&quot;, reflecting the fact that stearate forms a waxy solid |CITS: [16477801]|. Stearate was first described by Michel Eugene Chevreul in 1823. In cells |FRAME: STEARIC_ACID| is usually found in the form of |FRAME: Stearoyl-ACPs stearoyl-[acp]|, |FRAME: STEAROYL-COA| or |FRAME: Stearoyl-lipid &quot;incorporated into lipids&quot;|.</Comment>
